From 97302d5d8a7571782f3d1d96c1d9aa4bd009c827 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hubert Chao <hchao@chromium.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 07:53:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [Backport] CVE-2026-121174: Missing authorization in
 BackgroundFetch (1/2)

Manual cherry-pick of patch originally reviewed on
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8103044:
Enforce CORS in Background Fetch

Previously, the DownloadService previously defaulted all fetch requests
to `kNavigate` (which bypasses CORS). This CL updates the Background
Fetch delegate to pass the original fetch request mode down to the
DownloadService, so that the network service can correctly enforce CORS
checks on Background Fetch requests.

This additionally updates the proto serialization to persist the request
mode across browser restarts, and modify testing handlers to ensure the
CORS enforcement properly blocks requests that lack required
Access-Control headers. The browser tests have been updated to initiate
these fetches from the Window context instead of the Service Worker to
sidestep `kRestrictBackgroundFetchFromServiceWorker` restrictions.

Additionally, to prevent old suspended downloads from resuming and
bypassing CORS after the browser updates, EntryFromProto explicitly
upgrades the request mode to kCors if the fetch_request_mode is missing
and the client is BACKGROUND_FETCH.

The CORS enforcement and legacy proto upgrade logic are gated behind a
new `kBackgroundFetchCorsEnforcement` feature kill switch.

